.TH CWE-639 7 "2026-11-15" "CWE Top 25 (2025)" "Software Weakness Reference"
.SH NAME
CWE\-639 \- Authorization Bypass Through User\-Controlled Key (IDOR)
.SH RANK
2025 CWE Top 25 rank: \fB#24\fR (2024 rank: 30).
.SH CLASSIFICATION
Class: Access Control.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A user\-supplied key (e.g. a record ID) looks up a resource without verifying the user is authorized for that specific object. Changing the identifier (an 'insecure direct object reference') exposes other users' data.
.SH MITIGATION
Tie object lookups to the authenticated principal: filter by owner, or verify ownership after lookup and deny otherwise. Unpredictable identifiers help but never replace an authorization check.
.SH KNOWN EXPLOITED VULNERABILITIES
CISA KEV catalog (as of 2026-11-15): \fB1\fR exploited CVE mapped to this weakness. Each entry has the catalog write\-up of how it was used in the wild (not a runnable exploit) and the required remediation. Code\-level vulnerable\->fixed pairs, when curated, are under \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SS CVE\-2026\-55255
Langflow Langflow, added 2026\-07\-07.
.PP
Langflow Authorization Bypass Through User\-Controlled Key Vulnerability.
.PP
\fIHow it is exploited (CISA):\fR
Langflow contains an authorization bypass through user\-controlled key vulnerability which allows an authenticated attacker to execute any flow belonging to another user by specifying the victim's flow ID in the request.
.PP
\fIRemediation:\fR
Apply vendor mitigations; follow CISA BOD 26\-04 (risk\-based patching) and forensics triage. Discontinue use if no mitigation exists.
 Due 2026\-07\-10.
.PP
